          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which bacterial virulence factor is most critical for enabling E. coli to ascend</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which bacterial virulence factor is most critical for enabling E. coli to ascend from the bladder to the kidney and cause pyelonephritis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemolysin (cytotoxin that damages uroepithelium)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemolysin damages the urinary tract lining but does not help bacteria attach and ascend. It is a tissue-damage factor, not an adhesion factor.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urease enzyme</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Urease is produced by Proteus mirabilis, not E. coli. It alkalinises urine and promotes struvite stone formation rather than enabling ascent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>P fimbriae</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>P fimbriae bind specifically to glycolipid receptors on the uroepithelium of the renal pelvis and ureters, enabling E. coli to resist urinary flow and colonise the upper urinary tract.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Siderophores (iron-acquisition systems)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Siderophores help bacteria scavenge iron for growth but do not provide the adhesion needed for ascending infection to the kidney.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "P fimbriae bind to specific receptors on the uroepithelium of the upper urinary tract, allowing E. coli to resist urinary flushing and ascend to the kidney. Without P fimbriae, most E. coli strains remain confined to the bladder."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! P fimbriae are the key virulence factor distinguishing pyelonephritis-causing E. coli from strains confined to the lower urinary tract."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
