          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 22-year-old woman with mucopurulent cervical discharge tests positive for Chla</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 22-year-old woman with mucopurulent cervical discharge tests positive for Chlamydia trachomatis on NAAT, but her routine bacterial culture shows no growth. Which property of this organism best explains the negative culture?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It requires Thayer-Martin medium for growth</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thayer-Martin medium is a selective chocolate agar used for culturing Neisseria gonorrhoeae, not Chlamydia. Chlamydia cannot grow on any cell-free medium.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is an obligate intracellular organism</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chlamydia trachomatis can only replicate inside living host cells. It lacks the metabolic machinery to grow on any artificial culture medium, which is why NAAT or antigen-detection methods are essential for diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It requires anaerobic culture conditions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While some pathogens such as Clostridium require anaerobic conditions, Chlamydia's culture negativity is because it is an obligate intracellular pathogen, not because of oxygen sensitivity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It has a prolonged doubling time of several weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A very slow growth rate (as seen in Mycobacterium tuberculosis) would delay culture results but not render them permanently negative. Chlamydia cannot replicate outside living cells at all.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular organism — it can only replicate within host cells and lacks the capacity to grow on any artificial culture medium. Diagnosis therefore relies on NAAT or antigen-detection methods."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Chlamydia trachomatis is an obligate intracellular pathogen that depends entirely on host-cell energy and biosynthetic pathways for replication. NAAT is the gold standard for diagnosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
