          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 28-year-old pregnant woman in her second trimester has a routine urine culture</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 28-year-old pregnant woman in her second trimester has a routine urine culture growing ≥10⁵ CFU/mL of E. coli. She reports no urinary symptoms whatsoever. What is the most appropriate management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure and repeat the culture in the third trimester</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Delaying treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy is dangerous. Without treatment, up to 40% of cases progress to acute pyelonephritis, threatening both mother and foetus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Advise increased fluid intake and cranberry products</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While these measures may help prevent recurrent UTI in non-pregnant women, they are insufficient to manage confirmed bacteriuria in pregnancy. Active antibiotic treatment is mandatory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>No treatment is needed since she is asymptomatic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Unlike in non-pregnant adults, asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy carries a significant risk (up to 40%) of progressing to pyelonephritis. It is one of only two situations (alongside pre-urological surgery) where treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria is mandatory.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start appropriate antibiotic treatment</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy must always be treated. Hormonal dilatation of the ureters allows bacteria to ascend easily, and without antibiotics, up to 40% of cases progress to acute pyelonephritis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy must always be treated with antibiotics. Pregnancy causes hormonal dilatation of the ureters, making ascending infection far more likely, with up to 40% risk of progression to acute pyelonephritis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy must always be treated because the risk of progression to pyelonephritis is approximately 40% if left untreated."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
